0c7b6-101 | 2026-06-12T13:51:48Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | 1. **Blocking surface (92 sites, re-classified).** The naive "every refresh hits the network" reading is wrong: most `get_*`/`subscribe_*` core methods are pure nostrdb reads or sync interest registration (e.g. `client.rs:473`, `:542`, `:1514`, `:236`). The real wedges fall into three classes: \n    - **Class A — 360 s waits (5 sites):** `dispatch_action_for_result` waits on a correlation row with `NMP_PROTOCOL_ACTION_TIMEOUT = 360 s` (`nmp_runtime.rs:56`, `:774`). In-actor sites: blossom uploads (`blossom.rs:247` via nmp_app.rs:2288/2656/2976), NIP-29 chat publish (`chat.rs:101` via 3264), room join (`groups.rs:220` via 2911). Six-minute actor freeze each. \n    - **Class B — 65 s sign waits (~24 sites):** every publish helper signs through `NmpNostrSigner::sign_event` → `sign_unsigned_event` with `NMP_SIGN_TIMEOUT = 65 s` (`nmp_runtime.rs:54`, `:651`). Microseconds with a local key, up to 65 s per signature with a NIP-46 bunker — the exact V-90 class nmp-core fixed for itself. \n    - **Class C — 4–6 s bounded HTTP/fetch (3 sites):** NIP-11 probe, relay-list import, entity resolve. Survivable individually but stalls are serial per message. \n    - **Class D — ~58 local-only sites:** keep synchronous. \n  \n 2. **Framework intent is unambiguous.** The NMP repo's doctrine (D3/D8) forbids actor stalls; ADR-0024 prescribes the two-phase correlation-ID re-entry protocol; **ADR-0040 (Accepted 2026-05-31) closed the identical bug class (V-90) in nmp-core itself** with a serialized capability worker + `PendingSign` parked-op pattern; ADR-0031 is the worker-feeds-actor precedent. The framework explicitly provides the right primitive — highlighter's facade violates it, while ironically already implementing it correctly in nine bespoke workers (account-create, bunker sign-in, search, etc.). \n  \n RESULTS — chosen strategy: **hybrid (d)**, formalized as one `OpRunner` primitive: shared 2-thread tokio runtime, `OpDomain`-keyed in-flight registry with generation supersession + `AbortHandle`, a single `KernelMsg::OpResolved` variant, all state mutation staying on the actor thread (`apply_op_outcome`), 30 s worker-side deadlines, D6 toast/busy-flag error surfacing. Rejected: timeouts-only (proven insufficient by the 30 s account-create repro), and concurrent async handlers (breaks the exclusive `state.write()`-between-emits invariant). \n  \n NEXT — migration phases in the doc: Phase 0 (stall instrumentation + `block_on_local` wrapper + CI lint, 1–2 days), Phase 1 (top-10 wedge sites incl. all Class A, 4–6 days), Phase 2 (~24 publish helpers, 1.5–2 weeks), Phase 3 (`handle_core_delta` hardening), Phase 4 (fold nine legacy workers into `OpRunner`). Test strategy includes a deterministic dead-network harness — relay URLs are currently compile-time baked via `include_str!("relay_policy.json")` (`relays.rs:46-56`), so the design adds a `relay_policy_json` config seam plus a black-hole TCP listener and the framework's `nak serve` in-process relay precedent, with an account-creation regression test that fails on today's code by construction. \n  \n COMPLETED: Design document delivered; OpRunner hybrid strategy grounded in ADR-0040 precedent.
