84748-56 | 2026-06-13T13:12:51Z | session:847487cd-e15b-4222-85ee-4a5a2b6f590b |  \n The Android→core path is non-blocking. `viewModel::dispatch` (`HighlighterViewModel.kt:114-116`) → `HighlighterNmpApp::dispatch` (`core/src/nmp_app.rs:1812-1816`) only does `tx.try_send(...)` into a bounded channel. The action body — including every `block_on_local` (`refresh_home_feed`/`refresh_room_detail`) — runs on a dedicated background thread `highlighter-nmp-actor` (`nmp_app.rs:2513`). So the `block_on_local exceeded budget` warnings are on the actor thread, not the UI thread. \n  \n The real cause of "Skipped 200-322 frames"/ANR is a **recomposition flood**: the core's `emit` (`nmp_app.rs:11093`) calls `onState` synchronously (uncoalesced — `emit_hz` is not applied at this layer) with a full `HighlighterAppState` clone on every resolved op. `onState` (`HighlighterViewModel.kt:201-204`) sets the `StateFlow`; `MainActivity.kt:75` collects it, so a burst of card-hydration ops → burst of full-snapshot emits → whole-tree recompositions on Main. The per-card `LaunchedEffect`s (`HomeFeedPanel.kt:187,202,218,238,409,416`) don't check whether the datum is already in state, amplifying the burst. \n  \n Core threading constraint: mutations are already serialized through the single actor thread; `dispatch` is safe from any thread (just `try_send`), so the host must NOT serialize calls itself. \n 
