84748-89 | 2026-06-13T15:59:46Z | session:847487cd-e15b-4222-85ee-4a5a2b6f590b | ### Fix 2 — "Welcome to Highlighter / Dismiss" banner overlapping the top header \n  \n **What the banner is:** The `ToastBanner` composable in `RootScene.kt`, driven by `state.toast` (a `HighlighterToast` the core emits, e.g. with message "Welcome to Highlighter"). With `enableEdgeToEdge()` active in `MainActivity`, the outer `Box(Modifier.fillMaxSize())` starts at the raw screen top edge. The old `padding(16.dp)` + `Alignment.TopCenter` placed the banner at 16dp from the physical top — below the status bar but squarely over the `TopAppBar` rendered inside the `MainScaffold`'s `Scaffold`. \n  \n **Fix in `/Users/pablofernandez/Work/hl/app/android/app/src/main/java/com/highlighter/app/ui/RootScene.kt`:** \n - Added `WindowInsets`, `asPaddingValues`, `statusBars` imports \n - Toast container padding changed to `top = WindowInsets.statusBars + 64.dp + 8.dp`, `start/end = 16.dp` — the `64.dp` is the standard M3 `TopAppBar` height, the `8.dp` is a comfortable visual gap; this matches how iOS's `ShareToastBanner` is positioned below the navigation bar via `.overlay(alignment: .top)` + `.padding(.top, 8)` \n  [...]  \n `CoreError::NotFound`'s Display is literally `"not found"` (`core/src/errors.rs:20-21`). Three benign background per-card hydration paths surface it as a **global Error toast** via the raw error string: \n - `handle_web_metadata_resolved` — `nmp_app.rs:8969-8978` (web URL returns non-success/unparseable/cached-negative → `NotFound`, `web_metadata.rs:246,269,291`) \n - `handle_isbn_preview_resolved` — `nmp_app.rs:8898-8910` \n - `request_profile` subscribe failure — `nmp_app.rs:9536-9544` \n  \n These ops fire automatically per visible card, so any dead link/missing preview pops the banner on Highlights, Rooms, and Room detail. It's **persistent** because Android's `ToastBanner` (`Common.kt:118-142`) has no auto-expiry (only manual Dismiss), and new failures re-set it. iOS shows the same toast — its `ShareToastBanner` (`RootSceneView.swift:59-83`) also has no timer despite the misleading comment — confirming the emitter is shared/core-side. \n  \n **Fix (primary, core-side, fixes both platforms):** remove the `set_toast` calls in those three benign paths (log/insert a negative marker instead). **Secondary (Android safety net):** add a keyed `LaunchedEffect { delay; dispatch(ClearToast) }` in `RootScene.kt:169-186`. \n  \n **Confidence:** High on both root causes and the exact emitter/thread lines. The only file the other agent owns — `ui/rooms/RoomDetailPanel.kt` — is explicitly excluded from all proposed edits. [...]  \n - `handle_isbn_preview_resolved` (~line 8903): `Err` arm → `tracing::debug!(isbn = %requested, error = %message, "isbn preview not found")` \n - `handle_web_metadata_resolved` (~line 8971): `Err` arm → `tracing::debug!(url = %requested, error = %message, "web metadata not found")` \n - `request_profile` subscribe failure (~line 9536): → `tracing::debug!(pubkey = %pubkey_hex, error = %err, "profile subscription failed")` (the `changed = true` is preserved) \n  \n This fixes both Android and iOS since the emitter is shared. \n  \n ### Fix 2 secondary — Auto-expire toast (`RootScene.kt`) \n  \n **File:** `/Users/pablofernandez/Work/hl/app/android/app/src/main/java/com/highlighter/app/ui/RootScene.kt` \n  \n Added `import kotlinx.coroutines.delay`. Inside the `state.toast?.let { toast -> ... }` block (lines 169-186), added a `LaunchedEffect(toast)` keyed on the toast object that `delay(4_000)`s then dispatches `HighlighterAppAction.ClearToast`. Each new distinct toast resets the timer. \n  \n ### Verification results
84748-90 | 2026-06-13T15:59:50Z | session:847487cd-e15b-4222-85ee-4a5a2b6f590b |  \n ## Dominant root cause \n  \n **The `emit_hz` rate limiter was configured but never implemented.** The config accepted `emitHz = 30u` (line 49 of `HighlighterViewModel.kt`), passed it to `spawn_actor`, and the actor stored it in a `u32` parameter — but the actor loop's `KernelMsg::OpResolved` arm called `emit(...)` unconditionally on every resolved op (line 2555), with `emit_hz` only referenced in a dead `tracing::debug!` on actor exit (line 2583). There are 222 `emit(state, reconciler)` call sites in `handle_action` alone, plus one per `OpResolved`. During initial feed load, a burst of N concurrent op resolutions (profile + web-metadata + ISBN per card) fires N full-state clone+push sequences, each triggering a Compose recomposition. The `.sample(16.milliseconds)` on the Kotlin side reduces this but does not eliminate the OS-level side effect described below. \n  \n **Secondary cause confirmed: `onState` performed OS calls on the actor thread for every state emission.** `HighlighterViewModel.onState` (called on the Rust actor thread) unconditionally called `syncNetworkCallback(state.network.wifiOnlyEnabled)` on every emit. `syncNetworkCallback` calls `connectivityManager.registerNetworkCallback` / `unregisterNetworkCallback` / `getNetworkCapabilities` — expensive OS calls — regardless of whether `wifiOnlyEnabled` changed. With the emit storm, this meant hundreds of spurious OS calls per second from the wrong thread. \n  \n **Image loading is already async** — `RemoteImage` and `AvatarImage` both use Coil `AsyncImage` (`coil3.compose.AsyncImage`) with `crossfade(true)`. Not a cause. \n  \n **Hydration dispatch deduplication** was already applied (the `profiles.profileFor(pubkey) == null` guards at lines 188, 203, 220, 239, 410, 417 of `HomeFeedPanel.kt` are all present). Not a cause. \n  \n --- \n  \n ## Changes made \n  \n ### 1. `app/core/src/nmp_app.rs` — implement `emit_hz` rate limiter (Rust, full rebuild needed) \n  \n - **Line 12**: Added `RecvTimeoutError` to the existing `std::sync::mpsc` import. \n - **`spawn_actor` function** (formerly lines 2513–2586): Replaced the `while let Ok(msg) = rx.recv()` blocking loop with a `recv_timeout`-based loop that: \n   - Computes `op_emit_interval = 1000ms / emit_hz.clamp(1, 120)` (= 33ms at the configured 30 Hz). \n   - Tracks `last_op_emit: Instant` and `op_emit_dirty: bool`. \n   - On `KernelMsg::OpResolved`: applies the op outcome, then emits immediately if `last_op_emit.elapsed() >= op_emit_interval`, otherwise sets `op_emit_dirty = true` (trailing-emit path). \n   - Blocks on `recv_timeout(remaining)` where remaining is `op_emit_interval - elapsed` when dirty, or 1 hour (effectively blocking) when clean. \n   - On `RecvTimeoutError::Timeout`: flushes the dirty emit (trailing-emit guarantee — the latest state is always delivered within one interval). \n   - On `Action` / `CoreDelta`: those handlers already emit immediately via `handle_action`/`handle_core_delta`; after processing, resets `op_emit_dirty` and `last_op_emit` so the next interval is clean. \n   - Correctness: the final op result is always emitted (either immediately or on the first timeout after the burst), satisfying the trailing-emit requirement. \n 
