84748-127 | 2026-06-13T17:28:33Z | session:847487cd-e15b-4222-85ee-4a5a2b6f590b | --- \n  \n ## Dominant root cause \n  \n **The `emit_hz` rate limiter was configured but never implemented.** The config accepted `emitHz = 30u` (line 49 of `HighlighterViewModel.kt`), passed it to `spawn_actor`, and the actor stored it in a `u32` parameter — but the actor loop's `KernelMsg::OpResolved` arm called `emit(...)` unconditionally on every resolved op (line 2555), with `emit_hz` only referenced in a dead `tracing::debug!` on actor exit (line 2583). There are 222 `emit(state, reconciler)` call sites in `handle_action` alone, plus one per `OpResolved`. During initial feed load, a burst of N concurrent op resolutions (profile + web-metadata + ISBN per card) fires N full-state clone+push sequences, each triggering a Compose recomposition. The `.sample(16.milliseconds)` on the Kotlin side reduces this but does not eliminate the OS-level side effect described below. \n  \n **Secondary cause confirmed: `onState` performed OS calls on the actor thread for every state emission.** `HighlighterViewModel.onState` (called on the Rust actor thread) unconditionally called `syncNetworkCallback(state.network.wifiOnlyEnabled)` on every emit. `syncNetworkCallback` calls `connectivityManager.registerNetworkCallback` / `unregisterNetworkCallback` / `getNetworkCapabilities` — expensive OS calls — regardless of whether `wifiOnlyEnabled` changed. With the emit storm, this meant hundreds of spurious OS calls per second from the wrong thread. \n  \n **Image loading is already async** — `RemoteImage` and `AvatarImage` both use Coil `AsyncImage` (`coil3.compose.AsyncImage`) with `crossfade(true)`. Not a cause. \n  \n **Hydration dispatch deduplication** was already applied (the `profiles.profileFor(pubkey) == null` guards at lines 188, 203, 220, 239, 410, 417 of `HomeFeedPanel.kt` are all present). Not a cause. \n  \n --- \n  \n ## Changes made \n  \n ### 1. `app/core/src/nmp_app.rs` — implement `emit_hz` rate limiter (Rust, full rebuild needed) \n  \n - **Line 12**: Added `RecvTimeoutError` to the existing `std::sync::mpsc` import. \n - **`spawn_actor` function** (formerly lines 2513–2586): Replaced the `while let Ok(msg) = rx.recv()` blocking loop with a `recv_timeout`-based loop that: \n   - Computes `op_emit_interval = 1000ms / emit_hz.clamp(1, 120)` (= 33ms at the configured 30 Hz). \n   - Tracks `last_op_emit: Instant` and `op_emit_dirty: bool`. \n   - On `KernelMsg::OpResolved`: applies the op outcome, then emits immediately if `last_op_emit.elapsed() >= op_emit_interval`, otherwise sets `op_emit_dirty = true` (trailing-emit path). \n   - Blocks on `recv_timeout(remaining)` where remaining is `op_emit_interval - elapsed` when dirty, or 1 hour (effectively blocking) when clean. \n   - On `RecvTimeoutError::Timeout`: flushes the dirty emit (trailing-emit guarantee — the latest state is always delivered within one interval). \n   - On `Action` / `CoreDelta`: those handlers already emit immediately via `handle_action`/`handle_core_delta`; after processing, resets `op_emit_dirty` and `last_op_emit` so the next interval is clean. \n   - Correctness: the final op result is always emitted (either immediately or on the first timeout after the burst), satisfying the trailing-emit requirement.
