ader, RoomInvite: these are core-state-driven overlays (rendered when their state key is non-blank), meaning the calling screen already dispatched the Open action before the panel is even composed. No changes required. \n  \n ## Pull-to-refresh gestures intentionally left \n  \n `MainScaffold.kt` contains two `PullToRefreshBox` gesture wrappers: \n - Line 352-373: `HighlightsTab` — `onRefresh = { dispatch(RefreshHomeFeed) }` (pull gesture, not a button). \n - Line 389-417: `RoomsTab` — `onRefresh = { dispatch(RefreshRoomExplorer) }` (pull gesture, not a button). \n  \n Both are gesture-based (swipe-down) and were left in place per the task instructions. \n  \n ## Compile result \n  \n `./gradlew compileDebugKotlin` → **BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 6s** (1 task executed, 16 up-to-date, 0 errors, 0 warnings from the task itself). \n  [...] | Metric | Before (eager) | After (LazyColumn) | \n |---|---|---| \n | Janky frames | ~72% | **48.24%** | \n | Largest frame skip | 100–379 frames | **36 frames** | \n | Missed Vsync | (high) | 43 | \n | 50th / 90th pct | — | 36ms / 73ms | \n  \n **Verdict: jank materially improved** — janky frames dropped 24 percentage points, and the worst-case frame skip fell roughly 10x (from the 100–379 range to 36). Feed scrolling is smooth and tab navigation registers promptly. \n  \n The remaining ~48% is the expected next layer (heavy per-card content: remote images, hydration reads on visible cards) rather than the eager all-at-once realization that was the dominant cause — that root cause is now eliminated. If you want to push it further, the natural follow-ups would be image-load tuning and lightening the per-card composition, but the requested fix (eager `Column { forEach }` → single virtualized `LazyColumn`) is done and verified. \n  \n Assistant: Big milestone — the jank that was blocking navigation is fixed. On-device measurement after the lazy-list change: \n  \n | Metric | Before (eager) | After (LazyColumn) | \n |---|---|---| [...] **File:** `/Users/pablofernandez/Work/hl/app/android/app/src/main/java/com/highlighter/app/HighlighterViewModel.kt` \n  \n Added imports: `androidx.lifecycle.viewModelScope`, `SharingStarted`, `sample`, `stateIn`, `kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.milliseconds`. \n  \n Changed `state` from a direct `asStateFlow()` to a `by lazy` property that applies `.sample(16.milliseconds).stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.Eagerly, _state.value)`. The `@OptIn(FlowPreview::class)` annotation suppresses the API-preview warning. `onState` at line 201 is unchanged — it still writes `_state.value` directly from the core actor thread (thread-safe, no dispatch needed). `MainActivity.kt` calls `viewModel.state.collectAsState()` which works identically on the now-`StateFlow`-typed lazy property. \n  \n Coalescing approach: `sample(16 ms)` — drops all intermediate emissions during a burst and delivers only the latest value once per 16 ms window, bounding recomposition to ~1 per frame regardless of N simultaneous op resolutions. [...]  \n - Replaced the unconditional `android.util.Log.i(...)` call that built a `joinToString` string from the full `feed.items` list on every composition with a `Log.isLoggable(...)` guard. The `joinToString` allocation is now skipped in production (where the `highlighter-feed` tag is not at `INFO` level). \n  [...] `HighlightsTab` hosted `HomeFeedPanel` as a **single `LazyColumn` item**: `item { HomeFeedPanel(...) }`. This gave no virtualization benefit — all 140+ cards were composed simultaneously as part of one giant lazy item. \n  \n There was no `verticalScroll` Column wrapping it (that concern from the task description turned out not to apply here), but the result was identical: every card was eagerly composed at once. \n  \n ### HomeFeedPanel.kt — converted to LazyListScope extension \n  \n `HomeFeedPanel` was rewritten from a `@Composable fun` that returned a `Column` into a `LazyListScope` extension function named `homeFeedItems`. The LazyListScope now emits: \n  \n - `item(key = "feed_header")` — the `SectionHeader` + Refresh button \n - `item(key = "feed_error")` — optional error text (only when non-blank) \n - `item(key = "feed_loading")` — loading spinner with `testTag("feed_loading")` (when isLoading && empty) \n - `item(key = "feed_empty")` — EmptyPanel (when !loading && empty) \n - `items(feed.items, key = { it.stableId })` — one lazy item per feed card, using `stableId` (a `String` field present on `HighlighterHomeFeedItem`) \n  \n The key field used is `item.stableId` — confirmed from the generated UniFFI binding at line 4960 of `highlighter_core.kt`. \n  \n ### MainScaffold.kt — HighlightsTab updated \n  \n - Replaced `item { HomeFeedPanel(...) }` with `homeFeedItems(feed = state.homeFeed, dispatch = dispatch)` \n - Moved `testTag("feed_item_list")` to the `LazyColumn` itself (correct position per spec) \n - Added `verticalArrangement = Arrangement.spacedBy(10.dp)` to the `LazyColumn` (replaces the spacing that was inside the old inner `Column`) \n - Added `import androidx.compose.foundation.layout.Arrangement` \n - Updated import from `HomeFeedPanel` to `homeFeedItems` \n  [...] ## Feed card routing change \n  \n `HighlightFeedCard` is now `.clickable { onOpenDetail(leadHydrated) }` — tapping opens the **Highlight Detail screen**, not `OpenArticleReader`. The source reader (article/web) is reachable from *within* the detail via the tappable resource header. The `ReadingFeedCard` is unchanged (still dispatches `OpenArticleReader` directly — it's an article card, not a highlight card). \n 
