84748-142 | 2026-06-13T17:48:59Z | session:847487cd-e15b-4222-85ee-4a5a2b6f590b |  \n The existing code already bound `Preview` and `ImageCapture` together in a single `bindToLifecycle` call — that part was structurally correct and did NOT need restructuring. The actual bug was the missing camera fallback: on emulators that only expose a front camera, `DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA` throws `IllegalArgumentException: No available camera can be found`. The single `catch` block only logged and set an error message — it never retried. As a result, neither use case got bound to any camera, so when the shutter tapped `imageCapture.takePicture(...)`, CameraX threw the same error because the `ImageCapture` instance had never been successfully bound. \n  \n **Before (lines 144–155):** \n ```kotlin \n try { \n     cameraProvider.unbindAll() \n     cameraProvider.bindToLifecycle(lifecycleOwner, CameraSelector.DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA, preview, imageCapture) \n } catch (e: Exception) { \n     Log.e(TAG, "Camera bind failed", e) \n     errorMessage = "Camera failed to start: ${e.message}" \n } \n ``` \n  \n **After:** \n ```kotlin \n try { \n     cameraProvider.unbindAll() \n     cameraProvider.bindToLifecycle(lifecycleOwner, CameraSelector.DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA, preview, imageCapture) \n } catch (backE: Exception) { \n     Log.e(TAG, "Back camera bind failed, trying front camera", backE) \n     try { \n         cameraProvider.unbindAll() \n         cameraProvider.bindToLifecycle(lifecycleOwner, CameraSelector.DEFAULT_FRONT_CAMERA, preview, imageCapture) \n     } catch (frontE: Exception) { \n         Log.e(TAG, "Front camera bind also failed", frontE) \n         errorMessage = "Camera failed to start: ${frontE.message}" \n     } \n } \n ``` \n  \n ### ImageCapture lifecycle \n `ImageCapture` is created once via `remember { ImageCapture.Builder().build() }` in the composable scope (line 127) and bound alongside `Preview` in the single `bindToLifecycle` call inside the `AndroidView` factory's provider listener. The shutter lambda only calls `imageCapture.takePicture(...)` — no second `bindToLifecycle` anywhere. \n  \n ### Front/back fallback \n If `DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA` throws (emulator with only a front camera), the catch block calls `unbindAll()` again and retries with `DEFAULT_FRONT_CAMERA`, binding both `preview` and `imageCapture` in the same single call. A second catch handles the case where both selectors fail and surfaces the error in the UI. \n  \n ### Compile result \n `./gradlew compileDebugKotlin` → **BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 2s** — one task executed, no errors, only two pre-existing deprecation warnings unrelated to this change.
84748-143 | 2026-06-13T17:49:03Z | session:847487cd-e15b-4222-85ee-4a5a2b6f590b |  \n ### Gap 1 — CommentsPanel: Threaded Replies \n  \n **File changed:** `/Users/pablofernandez/Work/hl/app/android/app/src/main/java/com/highlighter/app/ui/comments/CommentsPanel.kt` (complete rewrite) \n  \n **Binding fields confirmed:** \n - `HighlighterCommentsSnapshot.topLevelEventIds: List<String>` — ordered list of root comment event IDs \n - `HighlighterCommentsSnapshot.childLinks: List<HighlighterCommentChildLinks>` — each entry has `.eventId` and `.childEventIds` \n - `HighlighterCommentsSnapshot.records: List<CommentRecord>` — full flat list; each `CommentRecord` has `.eventId`, `.pubkey`, `.body`, `.createdAt` \n - `HighlighterCommentInteraction` — `.eventId`, `.likeCount`, `.myLikeEventId`, `.isBookmarked` \n - `HighlighterCommentDraft.parentEventId: String?` — draft is keyed by parent event ID \n - `SetCommentDraft(parentEventId: String?, body: String)` — draft carries optional parentEventId \n - `PublishComment(parentEventId: String?)` — publishes to the parent or top-level \n  \n **How threading is built:** A `buildCommentTree()` function mirrors iOS `CommentTreeBuilder.build(snapshot:)` exactly — builds a `Map<String, CommentRecord>` from `records`, a `Map<String, List<String>>` from `childLinks`, then recursively assembles `CommentNode` trees by walking `topLevelEventIds`. The Rust core owns all ordering and orphan promotion; Kotlin only assembles view nodes. \n  \n **What was added:** \n - `CommentNode` data class (mirrors iOS `CommentNode`) \n - `buildCommentTree()` function (mirrors iOS `CommentTreeBuilder.build`) \n - `replyingToEventId` state in `CommentsPanel` — tracks which event is being replied to (null = top-level) \n - `ReplyContextBanner` — shows "Replying to @name" chip with a Cancel button when `replyingToEventId != null` \n - Composer label switches between "Add comment" and "Add reply" based on state \n - Thread display renders top-level nodes, then their first child indented (iOS `inlineReplyPreview` pattern), plus a "View N more replies" chip when there are additional children \n - `CommentRow` now has a Reply button; depth-1 rows get a 2dp thread rail on the left \n - `LaunchedEffect` in each row dispatches `RequestProfile(pubkey)` for avatar/name hydration (deduped by Rust) \n  \n **testTags added:** `comment_reply_button`, `comment_row`, `comment_reply_row` \n  \n **Note:** The snapshot does not carry a `parentEventId` field directly on `CommentRecord` (it uses `parentTagName`/`parentTagValue` for NIP-22 e/q tag references). The Rust core already resolves this into `topLevelEventIds` + `childLinks`, which is exactly what the tree builder uses — no gap. \n  [...] User: [Agent task result: Agent "Add highlight detail screen + comments entry" completed] \n Here is the complete report: \n  \n --- \n  \n ## Approach \n  \n **Host-side local navigation** — `var selectedHighlight by remember { mutableStateOf<HydratedHighlight?>(null) }` in `HighlightsTab` inside `MainScaffold.kt`. When the user taps a card it sets this state; when non-null, `DestinationScaffold` renders `HighlightDetailScreen` in place of the feed (same `BackHandler`/`DestinationScaffold` pattern used by Settings, Capture, Bookmarks, Feedback). No new `ScaffoldRoute` enum value needed — the highlight is already present in the feed item, so no core state or action round-trip is required. The existing core-state overlays (Comments, Profile, ArticleReader) layer on top of this exactly as before via `RootScene`. \n  \n --- \n  \n ## Files changed \n  \n | File | What changed | \n |---|---| \n | `ui/home/HighlightDetailScreen.kt` | **New file** — the full detail screen | \n | `ui/home/HomeFeedPanel.kt` | Added `onOpenHighlightDetail` callback; made `HighlightFeedCard` Surface clickable to dispatch it (instead of `OpenArticleReader`) | \n | `ui/MainScaffold.kt` | Added `selectedHighlight` state + `BackHandler` + `DestinationScaffold` host in `HighlightsTab`; passes `onOpenHighlightDetail` down to `homeFeedItems` | \n  \n --- \n  \n ## Actions wired (all confirmed from binding) \n  \n - **Comment**: `HighlighterAppAction.OpenComments(rootTagName="e", rootTagValue=highlight.eventId, rootKind=9802u)` — exact tag a kind:9802 highlight uses; opens the existing threaded `CommentsPanel` via the core overlay stack in `RootScene`. \n - **Author byline tap**: `HighlighterAppAction.OpenProfile(pubkeyHex=highlight.pubkey)` → `OpenProfile(pubkeyHex)`. \n - **Source header tap (article)**: `HighlighterAppAction.OpenArticleReader(pubkeyHex, dTag, null)` — extracted from `"30023:<pubkey>:<dTag>"` address. Only shown when the address parses correctly. \n - **Bookmark**: `HighlighterAppAction.ToggleArticleBookmark(address)` — only for 30023 article-backed highlights (iOS parity: omitted/invisible for web/book/unknown). \n - **Share**: Android system share sheet (`Intent.ACTION_SEND`) with `https://beta.highlighter.com/highlight/<eventId>` — mirrors iOS `ShareLink(item: url)`. \n  \n --- \n  \n ## Feed card routing change \n  \n `HighlightFeedCard` is now `.clickable { onOpenDetail(leadHydrated) }` — tapping opens the **Highlight Detail screen**, not `OpenArticleReader`. The source reader (article/web) is reachable from *within* the detail via the tappable resource header. The `ReadingFeedCard` is unchanged (still dispatches `OpenArticleReader` directly — it's an article card, not a highlight card). \n  \n --- \n  \n ## Omitted (no binding) \n  \n - **ShareHighlightRepost** (`ShareHighlightRepost(eventId, authorPubkeyHex, relayHint, targetGroupId)`) requires a `targetGroupId` (room picker). Android has no room-picker sheet for highlights (iOS has `ShareToCommunitySheet`). Omitted — the system share fills the affordance instead. \n - **Web reader** from source header: no `OpenWebReader` or equivalent action in the binding; iOS dispatches to a `WebReaderTarget` navigation destination. Web URLs are recognized but the tap does nothing (o