84748-163 | 2026-06-13T18:02:16Z | session:847487cd-e15b-4222-85ee-4a5a2b6f590b |  \n The Android→core path is non-blocking. `viewModel::dispatch` (`HighlighterViewModel.kt:114-116`) → `HighlighterNmpApp::dispatch` (`core/src/nmp_app.rs:1812-1816`) only does `tx.try_send(...)` into a bounded channel. The action body — including every `block_on_local` (`refresh_home_feed`/`refresh_room_detail`) — runs on a dedicated background thread `highlighter-nmp-actor` (`nmp_app.rs:2513`). So the `block_on_local exceeded budget` warnings are on the actor thread, not the UI thread. \n  \n The real cause of "Skipped 200-322 frames"/ANR is a **recomposition flood**: the core's `emit` (`nmp_app.rs:11093`) calls `onState` synchronously (uncoalesced — `emit_hz` is not applied at this layer) with a full `HighlighterAppState` clone on every resolved op. `onState` (`HighlighterViewModel.kt:201-204`) sets the `StateFlow`; `MainActivity.kt:75` collects it, so a burst of card-hydration ops → burst of full-snapshot emits → whole-tree recompositions on Main. The per-card `LaunchedEffect`s (`HomeFeedPanel.kt:187,202,218,238,409,416`) don't check whether the datum is already in state, amplifying the burst. \n  \n Core threading constraint: mutations are already serialized through the single actor thread; `dispatch` is safe from any thread (just `try_send`), so the host must NOT serialize calls itself. \n  \n **Fix (Android-side):** (1a) `sample`/conflate the collected `StateFlow` (`HighlighterViewModel.kt:58-59`) to coalesce bursts to ~1 recomposition/frame — high confidence this resolves the ANR; (1b) guard each hydration dispatch on absence from `LocalProfiles`/`LocalIsbnPreviews`/`LocalWebMetadata` in `HomeFeedPanel.kt`. Do NOT wrap `onState` in `Dispatchers.Main`.
84748-164 | 2026-06-13T18:02:16Z | session:847487cd-e15b-4222-85ee-4a5a2b6f590b | Added imports: `androidx.lifecycle.viewModelScope`, `SharingStarted`, `sample`, `stateIn`, `kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.milliseconds`. \n  \n Changed `state` from a direct `asStateFlow()` to a `by lazy` property that applies `.sample(16.milliseconds).stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.Eagerly, _state.value)`. The `@OptIn(FlowPreview::class)` annotation suppresses the API-preview warning. `onState` at line 201 is unchanged — it still writes `_state.value` directly from the core actor thread (thread-safe, no dispatch needed). `MainActivity.kt` calls `viewModel.state.collectAsState()` which works identically on the now-`StateFlow`-typed lazy property. \n  \n Coalescing approach: `sample(16 ms)` — drops all intermediate emissions during a burst and delivers only the latest value once per 16 ms window, bounding recomposition to ~1 per frame regardless of N simultaneous op resolutions.
84748-165 | 2026-06-13T18:02:16Z | session:847487cd-e15b-4222-85ee-4a5a2b6f590b | ### 1. `app/core/src/nmp_app.rs` — implement `emit_hz` rate limiter (Rust, full rebuild needed) \n  \n - **Line 12**: Added `RecvTimeoutError` to the existing `std::sync::mpsc` import. \n - **`spawn_actor` function** (formerly lines 2513–2586): Replaced the `while let Ok(msg) = rx.recv()` blocking loop with a `recv_timeout`-based loop that: \n   - Computes `op_emit_interval = 1000ms / emit_hz.clamp(1, 120)` (= 33ms at the configured 30 Hz). \n   - Tracks `last_op_emit: Instant` and `op_emit_dirty: bool`. \n   - On `KernelMsg::OpResolved`: applies the op outcome, then emits immediately if `last_op_emit.elapsed() >= op_emit_interval`, otherwise sets `op_emit_dirty = true` (trailing-emit path). \n   - Blocks on `recv_timeout(remaining)` where remaining is `op_emit_interval - elapsed` when dirty, or 1 hour (effectively blocking) when clean. \n   - On `RecvTimeoutError::Timeout`: flushes the dirty emit (trailing-emit guarantee — the latest state is always delivered within one interval). \n   - On `Action` / `CoreDelta`: those handlers already emit immediately via `handle_action`/`handle_core_delta`; after processing, resets `op_emit_dirty` and `last_op_emit` so the next interval is clean. \n   - Correctness: the final op result is always emitted (either immediately or on the first timeout after the burst), satisfying the trailing-emit requirement. \n 
