ed) \n  \n - **Line 12**: Added `RecvTimeoutError` to the existing `std::sync::mpsc` import. \n - **`spawn_actor` function** (formerly lines 2513–2586): Replaced the `while let Ok(msg) = rx.recv()` blocking loop with a `recv_timeout`-based loop that: \n   - Computes `op_emit_interval = 1000ms / emit_hz.clamp(1, 120)` (= 33ms at the configured 30 Hz). \n   - Tracks `last_op_emit: Instant` and `op_emit_dirty: bool`. \n   - On `KernelMsg::OpResolved`: applies the op outcome, then emits immediately if `last_op_emit.elapsed() >= op_emit_interval`, otherwise sets `op_emit_dirty = true` (trailing-emit path). \n   - Blocks on `recv_timeout(remaining)` where remaining is `op_emit_interval - elapsed` when dirty, or 1 hour (effectively blocking) when clean. \n   - On `RecvTimeoutError::Timeout`: flushes the dirty emit (trailing-emit guarantee — the latest state is always delivered within one interval). \n   - On `Action` / `CoreDelta`: those handlers already emit immediately via `handle_action`/`handle_core_delta`; after processing, resets `op_emit_dirty` and `last_op_emit` so the next interval is clean. \n   - Correctness: the final op result is always emitted (either immediately or on the first timeout after the burst), satisfying the trailing-emit requirement. \n  \n ### 2. `app/android/app/src/main/java/com/highlighter/app/HighlighterViewModel.kt` — guard OS calls in `onState`
