cents(limit: 24) }`. \n - While loading, shows a `CircularProgressIndicator`. When books arrive they render in a `LazyRow` of 80dp `RecentBookCard` columns (2:3 cover + title). Tapping commits `HighlighterCaptureArtifact.Existing(book)` as `selectedArtifact`. \n - Search mode (non-blank query) hides recents and shows the existing `ArtifactPickerRow` list from `bookPicker.searchResults`, also committing via `Existing`. \n  \n **Manual ISBN entry + lookup (flow #26)** \n - A separate "Manual ISBN" labelled `OutlinedTextField` (`testTag: capture_isbn_field`) with a "Find" button. \n - On tap, calls the `normalizeIsbn(raw)` free function (returns `String?`). If null → shows `isbnError` as `supportingText` ("Enter a valid 10- or 13-digit ISBN"). \n - Dedup: if the normalized `isbn:$normalized` `catalogId` matches any recent book, commits that `Existing` record directly — same shortcut as iOS `beginResolve()`. \n - Otherwise dispatches `RequestIsbnPreview(normalized)` and sets `resolvingIsbn`. \n - `resolvedPreview` is reactively derived from `LocalIsbnPreviews.current.previewForIsbn(resolvingIsbn)` — re-evaluated on every recomposition when NMP delivers the preview. \n - `IsbnPreviewCard` (tagged `capture_isbn_preview`) shows a spinner while `preview == null`, or cover + title + author once it arrives. \n - "Use" button (`testTag: capture_isbn_use`) calls `preview.copy(source="book", catalogId="isbn:$isbn", catalogKind="isbn", referenceTagName="i", referenceTagValue=catalogId, referenceKind="isbn", highlightTagName="i", highlightTagValue=catalogId, highlightReferenceKey="i:$catalogId")` — mirroring iOS `ISBNPreviewSheet.commit()` exactly — then commits `HighlighterCaptureArtifact.Pending(committed)` as `selectedArtifact`. \n  \n **Publish path** \n - `selectedArtifact` is now `HighlighterCaptureArtifact?` (was `ArtifactRecord?`), passed directly to `PublishCaptureHighlight` and `PublishCapturePicture` — both already accept `HighlighterCaptureArtifact` / optional respectively, so no action-signature changes were needed. \n  \n --- \n  \n ### testTags added \n  \n | Tag | Element | \n |---|---| \n | `capture_book_recents` | `LazyRow` containing recent book cards | \n | `capture_recent_book` | Each `RecentBookCard` `Column` | \n | `capture_isbn_field` | Manual ISBN `OutlinedTextField` | \n | `capture_isbn_preview` | `IsbnPreviewCard` `Card` | \n | `capture_isbn_use` | "Use" `Button` inside preview card | \n  \n --- \n  \n ### Adaptations / nothing invented \n  \n - `HighlighterBookPickerSnapshot` has `isLoadingRecents` and `isSearching` booleans — both confirmed present and used. \n - `ArtifactPreview` is a `data class` with `.copy()` available — used it for the ISBN commit instead of constructing the full constructor call. \n - No symbols were invented. All names are from the grepped binding. \n  \n --- \n  \n ### compileDebugKotlin result \n  \n `BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 580ms` — zero errors, zero warnings. No APK built, no emulator launched. \n  \n --- \n  \n **Camera + on-device OCR (Phase 4b) is still pending.** \n  [...] User: [Agent task result: Agent "OCR Phase 4b: camera + ML Kit OCR + barcode" completed] \n `compileDebugKotlin` passes cleanly. Here is the full report. \n  \n --- \n  \n ## Phase 4b — OCR Capture Implementation Report \n  \n ### Dependencies added (`app/android/app/build.gradle.kts`) \n | Artifact | Version | \n |---|---| \n | `androidx.camera:camera-camera2` | 1.4.2 | \n | `androidx.camera:camera-lifecycle` | 1.4.2 | \n | `androidx.camera:camera-view` | 1.4.2 | \n | `com.google.mlkit:text-recognition` | 16.0.1 | \n | `com.google.mlkit:barcode-scanning` | 17.3.0 | \n  \n All are on-device; ML Kit text recognition and barcode scanning run without network on the emulator's virtual camera scene. \n  \n ### Manifest change \n `android.permission.CAMERA` added to `AndroidManifest.xml`. The runtime permission is requested via `rememberLauncherForActivityResult(RequestPermission)` inside `CapturePanel` before opening the camera viewfinder. \n  \n --- \n  \n ### Files created (4 new, 1 rewritten) \n  \n #### `CameraCapture.kt` — camera viewfinder + OCR pipeline \n - `CameraCaptureScreen`: full-screen CameraX `PreviewView` bound to `DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA` with a shutter button. On capture, calls `captureAndOcr()`. \n - `captureAndOcr()`: suspending coroutine — takes photo via `ImageCapture.OnImageCapturedCallback`, applies rotation, JPEG-encodes at quality 90, runs `recognizeLines()` to get `List<OcrLine>`, calls `OcrStructureReconstructor.toMarkdown()` + `flattenForAlt()`, returns `CaptureResult(jpegBytes, width, height, ocrMarkdown, altText, lines)`. \n - `recognizeLines()`: wraps ML Kit `TextRecognition.getClient(TextRecognizerOptions.DEFAULT_OPTIONS)` as a coroutine; converts each `TextBlock.Line` bounding box to an `OcrLine` with pixel-coordinate top/bottom/left/right. \n - testTags: `capture_camera_button` (dismiss/back), `capture_take_photo` (shutter button). \n  \n #### `OcrStructureReconstructor.kt` — markdown reconstruction \n Direct Kotlin port of iOS `OCRStructureReconstructor.swift` + `OCRLine`/`OCRWord` structs: \n - `OcrLine` data class with `top/bottom/left/right/midY/height/width/midX`. [...] User: [Agent task result: Agent "Fix CameraX capture binding bug" completed] \n BUILD SUCCESSFUL. Only pre-existing deprecation warnings (not introduced by this change), no errors. \n  \n --- \n  \n ## Summary \n  \n ### Before vs After — the binding \n  \n The existing code already bound `Preview` and `ImageCapture` together in a single `bindToLifecycle` call — that part was structurally correct and did NOT need restructuring. The actual bug was the missing camera fallback: on emulators that only expose a front camera, `DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA` throws `IllegalArgumentException: No available camera can be found`. The single `catch` block only logged and set an error message — it never retried. As a result, neither use case got bound to any camera, so when the shutter tapped `imageCapture.takePicture(...)`, CameraX threw the same error because the `ImageCapture` instance had never been successfully bound. \n  \n **Before (lines 144–155):** \n ```kotlin \n try { \n     cameraProvider.unbindAll() \n     cameraProvider.bindToLifecycle(lifecycleOwner, CameraSelector.DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA, preview, imageCapture) \n } catch (e: Exception) { \n     Log.e(TAG, "Camera bind failed", e) \n     errorMessage = "Camera failed to start: ${e.message}" \n } \n ``` \n  \n **After:** \n ```kotlin \n try { \n     cameraProvider.unbindAll() \n     cameraProvider.bindToLifecycle(lifecycleOwner, CameraSelector.DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA, preview, imageCapture) \n } catch (backE: Exception) { \n     Log.e(TAG, "Back camera bind failed, trying front camera", backE) \n     try { \n         cameraProvider.unbindAll() \n         cameraProvider.bindToLifecycle(lifecycleOwner, CameraSelector.DEFAULT_FRONT_CAMERA, preview, imageCapture) \n     } catch (frontE: Exception) { \n         Log.e(TAG, "Front camera bind also failed", frontE) \n         errorMessage = "Camera failed to start: ${frontE.message}" \n     } \n } \n ``` \n  \n ### ImageCapture lifecycle \n `ImageCapture` is created once via `remember { ImageCapture.Builder().build() }` in the composable scope (line 127) and bound alongside `Preview` in the single `bindToLifecycle` call inside the `AndroidView` factory's provider listener. The shutter lambda only calls `imageCapture.takePicture(...)` — no second `bindToLifecycle` anywhere. \n  \n ### Front/back fallback \n If `DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA` throws (emulator with only a front camera), the catch block calls `unbindAll()` again and retries with `DEFAULT_FRONT_CAMERA`, binding both `preview` and `imageCapture` in the same single call. A second catch handles the case where both selectors fail and surfaces the error in the UI. \n  \n ### Compile result \n `./gradlew compileDebugKotlin` → **BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 2s** — one task executed, no errors, only two pre-existing deprecation warnings unrelated to this change. \n  [...] User: [Agent task result: Agent "Fix capture review infinite-height crash" completed] \n BUILD SUCCESSFUL. Here is the complete report: \n  \n --- \n  \n **Exact nesting that caused the crash** \n  \n - **Outer scroll:** `MainScaffold.kt` lines 139-155 — the `ScaffoldRoute.CAPTURE` branch renders `CapturePanel` as a single `item` inside a `LazyColumn(Modifier.fillMaxSize())`. A `LazyColumn` is a vertically-scrollable container and provides **unbounded (infinite) height** to its items. \n - **Inner scroll:** `CapturePageReview.kt` lines 75-79 — the root `Column` of `CapturePageReview` had `Modifier.verticalScroll(rememberScrollState())`. When the Review phase is active, `CapturePanelReviewWrapper` renders `CapturePageReview` directly, making this `verticalScroll Column` a direct descendant of the outer `LazyColumn` item — the classic "vertically scrollable component measured with infinity maximum height constraints" crash. \n  \n **Fix applied** \n  \n Removed `Modifier.verticalScroll(rememberScrollState())` from the root `Column` in `/Users/pablofernandez/Work/hl/app/android/app/src/main/java/com/highlighter/app/ui/capture/CapturePageReview.kt` (and the now-unused `rememberScrollState`/`verticalScroll` imports). The outer `LazyColumn` in `MainScaffold` already handles all vertical scrolling for the Capture route — `CapturePageReview`'s `Column` only needs its `padding(16.dp)` modifier. \n  \n **Scroll container confirmation** \n  \n The review screen content — page image thumbnail, upload indicator, OCR `SelectionContainer` text (tagged `capture_ocr_text`), quote `OutlinedTextField` (tagged `capture_select_quote`), and the action row with "Use as quote" / "Retake" buttons — are all children of a plain (non-scrolling) `Column`, which is a single `item` inside the enclosing `LazyColumn`. There is exactly one vertical scroll container for the screen, with properly bounded intrinsic height for all children. All testTags and OCR/quote/publish logic are untouched. \n  \n **Compile result** \n  \n `./gradlew compileDebugKotlin` → `BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 1s` (1 executed, 16 up-to-date). \n  [...] Three-phase internal enum: `Idle → Camera → Review`. From `Idle`: \n - "Camera" button checks/requests the runtime CAMERA permission, then enters `Camera` phase. \n - "Photo" button retains the existing gallery-picker path. \n - ISBN row now has a "Scan" `OutlinedButton` (testTag `capture_scan_barcode`) next to "Find"; tapping it shows `IsbnBarcodeScannerScreen` as an in-panel overlay; detected ISBN feeds straight into the existing `RequestIsbnPreview` / preview-card flow. \n  \n In `Camera` phase: `CameraCaptureScreen` is shown full-screen. On capture, `UploadCapturePhoto(bytes, "image/jpeg", width, height, altText)` is dispatched immediately (upload runs in parallel with review), then transitions to `Review` phase. \n  \n In `Review` phase (`CapturePanelReviewWrapper`): \n - Step 1 (`CapturePageReview`): image + OCR text, quote selection → saves `quote` + `context`. \n - Step 2 (publish metadata): quote editor, note, compact recent-books row, community picker. \n - Publish button dispatches `PublishCaptureHighlight(artifact, selectedGroupId, HighlightDraft(...))` with: \n   ``` \n   HighlightDraft( \n     quote       = quote.trim(), \n     context     = context.trim(),    // paragraph surrounding the selection \n     note        = note.trim(), \n     clipStartSeconds = null, \n     clipEndSeconds   = null, \n     clipSpeaker      = "", \n     clipTranscriptSegmentIds = emptyList(), \n     image = BlossomUpload(          // refreshed alt mirrors iOS imageWithAlt \n       url, sha256Hex, mime, sizeBytes, width, height, \n       alt = OcrStructureReconstructor.flattenForAlt(captureResult.ocrMarkdown) \n     ) \n   ) \n   ``` \n   This exactly mirrors iOS `CaptureStore.publish()` — confirmed by reading `CaptureStore.swift` lines 258–283. \n  \n Pre-fills selected book from `bookPicker.recentBooks.first()` (mirrors iOS `prefillRecentBook()`). \n  \n --- \n 
